EXIT(2)                        P32W Unix                         EXIT(2)

NAME
       exit, _exit - terminate a process

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void exit(int status);
       void _exit(int status);

DESCRIPTION
       _exit is the system call. It ends the calling process: every
       open file is closed, the current directory is released, and
       the low 8 bits of status are kept for the parent, which
       learns them through wait(2) (as status << 8). The process
       becomes a zombie until its parent waits for it. Its children,
       if any, are given to init (process 1), which waits for them.
       _exit never returns.

       exit is the C library's: it calls the functions registered
       with atexit (last registered first), flushes every stdio
       output stream (stdio(3)), then calls _exit. Returning from
       main calls exit with main's return value.

       By convention status 0 means success and anything else
       failure.

RETURN VALUE
       Neither returns.

SEE ALSO
       wait(2), fork(2), signal(2), stdlib(3), stdio(3)

BUGS
       The parent gets no signal when a child exits (there is no
       SIGCHLD). Only 8 bits of the status survive.
